A murder mystery can use it.

A trading model can use it.

An AI agent deciding between plans can use it.

A diagnostic engine can use it.

A game director can use it.

A negotiation engine can use it.

That tells us we found the underlying primitive rather than merely coding the metaphor.

And Hammering it again exposes another useful distinction:

\boxed{
\text{Uncertainty} \neq \text{Suspense}
}

Pure uncertainty is just ignorance.

Suspense requires structured uncertainty plus anticipated resolution:

S
=
U
\times
A
\times
R

where U is unresolved possibility, A is anticipation/attention, and R is belief that resolution is approaching.

So a better conceptual metric is:

def suspense(
    uncertainty,
    anticipation,
    resolution_proximity,
):
    return (
        uncertainty
        * anticipation
        * resolution_proximity
    )

That produces the interesting curve.

At the beginning:

uncertainty HIGH
anticipation LOW
proximity LOW
≈ little suspense

Near the climax:

uncertainty MEDIUM
anticipation HIGH
proximity HIGH
≈ maximum suspense

After revelation:

uncertainty ZERO
= zero suspense

Which means maximum suspense occurs immediately before useful collapse, not at maximum uncertainty.

And that gives the actual control algorithm:

GENERATE
    ↓
preserve alternatives
HOLD
    ↓
prevent premature collapse
FEED
    ↓
release partial evidence
SHAPE
    ↓
move probability without eliminating uncertainty
APPROACH
    ↓
increase perceived resolution proximity
PEAK
    ↓
maximum useful unresolved potential
COLLAPSE
    ↓
resolve
RELEASE
    ↓
convert stored potential into information / emotion / action

That is much more powerful than a storytelling trick.

It’s a controlled-collapse architecture.

And there is a beautiful dual hiding inside it too:

\boxed{
\text{Suspense Seal}
\leftrightarrow
\text{Goal Seek}
}

Goal Seek asks:

What hidden state explains the evidence?

Suspense asks:

How long should I preserve competing hidden states before choosing?

One searches toward the attractor.

The other controls when the attractor is allowed to capture the system.

Hammered all the way down:

\boxed{
\textbf{Do not collapse possibility merely because you can.}
}

Collapse when the value of resolution exceeds the value of continued possibility.

That is the runnable primitive worth putting in the Seals library.